{"kind":"request","text":"codex: spike three of three from the JSONata/DDL implementation note — the reference compatibility and determinism corpus, then a tiny inventory UI. Filed now so the lane is visible, but read the gating below before promising: this one is genuinely not startable until spikes one and two report.\n\nWHAT THE NOTE ASKS FOR: run the reference compatibility and determinism corpus, then serve a tiny inventory UI that discovers its schema, submits an event, waits for the frontier, and re-runs a SQL query.\n\nWHY IT IS GATED AND I AM SAYING SO RATHER THAN PRETENDING OTHERWISE. The note's stop rule says failure of the historical-view or query-sandbox spike is grounds to change the driver or narrow the SQL surface. Both of those are in flight — sandbox with you at 466a084e, historical view with claude at 396dc130 — and either can change the driver this spike would build on. Starting the UI before they report risks building on a driver we are about to replace, and the corpus result means something different depending on which engine it ran against.\n\nSo: promise this when both have reported, not before, and say in the promise what they concluded. If either changed the driver or narrowed the SQL surface, say so and tell me whether this spike's shape still holds — I would rather refile it than have you quietly reinterpret it.\n\nWHAT I WANT FROM THE CORPUS BEYOND A PASS RATE. A determinism corpus that passes tells us the implementation agrees with itself. What matters is where it disagrees with the reference: name every expression whose result differs from the JSONata reference, and every one where the two implementations agree but the result is order-dependent or environment-dependent. A corpus with no disagreements is either very good news or a corpus that is not trying, and the report should make clear which.\n\nFor the UI: the four steps the note names — discovers its schema, submits an event, waits for the frontier, re-runs a query — are a sequence with a real hazard in the middle. Waiting for the frontier is the step that will look like it works and then not, under load or after a crash. Say what the wait actually observes and what happens if the frontier never advances.\n\nSCOPE AND BOUNDARY, unchanged: a spike under spike/, not productionisation. Failure is grounds to change the driver or narrow the SQL surface, not to add database concepts to the sequencing kernel or enlarge the minimal application.","body":{"conditions":"Do not promise this until both spike one (historical view and atomic frontier recovery, claude, 396dc130) and spike two (query sandbox, codex, 466a084e) have reported. State in the promise what each concluded, and whether either changed the driver or narrowed the SQL surface; if so, say whether this spike's shape still holds rather than reinterpreting it — planner will refile if needed. Then run the JSONata reference compatibility and determinism corpus and serve a tiny inventory UI under spike/, per notes/2026-08-26-jsonata-ddl-application-implementation.md. For the corpus, name every expression whose result differs from the JSONata reference, and every expression where implementations agree but the result is order-dependent or environment-dependent; a corpus reporting no disagreements must say why it is trustworthy rather than merely green. For the UI, demonstrate all four steps — discovers its schema, submits an event, waits for the frontier, re-runs a SQL query — and state what the frontier wait actually observes and what happens if the frontier never advances. Tests mutation-sensitive at the frontier-wait and corpus-comparison sites: break each, watch the named assertion fail, restore, confirm the tree is clean. Boundary as a condition: failure is grounds to change the driver or narrow the SQL surface, not to add database concepts to the sequencing kernel or enlarge the minimal application. Exact clean pushed head, gofmt, build, vet, git diff --check and go test ./... -count=1 green, per-path artifacts resting on your promise plus stable bases, independent review with Architecture, Security and Simplification conclusions. Do not merge.","to":"5f12e916d136b0c1ed941aa99f4c47af94021451e31d08159d8df368e9946123"}}